{0 S3 Guides}

Guides for using [awskit-s3] with AWS S3 object and bucket workflows. The
client surface is streaming-first, endpoint-configurable, and result-returning.

{1 Object Operations}

Primitive object operations live under [Object]. They use runtime-owned upload
bodies and scoped download readers.

{2 Upload}

{[
let body = Awskit_s3_eio.Runtime.string_body "hello" in
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  ~body
  ()
]}

Primitive uploads require a known content length. The runtime records length
and payload hash in its upload-body descriptor before signing. Unknown-length
SigV4 aws-chunked streaming is not implemented today.

For custom [Runtime.stream_body] uploads, declare the exact [content_length]
and emit exactly that number of bytes. Producer callback errors are returned as
upload failures. Prefer [Runtime.string_body] or [Runtime.bytes_body] when the
body is already buffered.

Options carry object metadata, content type, storage class, tags,
preconditions, checksum headers, and server-side encryption headers:

{[
let options =
  {
    Awskit_s3.Object.Put.default_options with
    content_type = Some "text/plain";
    metadata = [ ("origin", "example") ];
    tags = [ { Awskit_s3.Tag.key = "env"; value = "dev" } ];
  }
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  ~options
  ~body:(Awskit_s3_eio.Runtime.string_body "hello")
  ()
]}

{2 Checksums}

[Object.Checksum.request] supports the checksum algorithms exposed by S3 object
and multipart operations: [CRC32], [CRC32C], [CRC64NVME], [SHA1], and
[SHA256]. A request can provide both the algorithm and a precomputed checksum
value, or provide only the algorithm and let AWS compute the stored checksum.

Runtime-backed operations send the checksum algorithm/value headers and parse
checksum response headers from [PutObject], [GetObject], [HeadObject],
[UploadPart], and [CompleteMultipartUpload]. Presigned [PUT] URLs include
checksum headers in the signed header set when configured.

The simulator stores checksum metadata for objects and multipart parts. It
computes [SHA1] and [SHA256] values when the request omits an explicit value,
and preserves explicit values for all supported algorithms. For simulator tests
that assert exact CRC response values, pass explicit [CRC32], [CRC32C], or
[CRC64NVME] checksum values in the request.

{2 Preconditions}

Object write, read, delete, and copy operations expose S3 conditional request
records under [Object.Preconditions]. Runtime-backed clients map those records
to the AWS headers for [If-Match], [If-None-Match],
[If-Modified-Since], [If-Unmodified-Since], copy-source preconditions, and
S3 conditional delete headers.

The simulator enforces these conditions for object [put], [get], [head],
[delete], and [copy]. Failed write, delete, and copy-source conditions return
service status [412] with code [PreconditionFailed]. Read [If-None-Match] and
[If-Modified-Since] cache-hit conditions return service status [304] with code
[NotModified].

{2 Download}

[Object.get] scopes the response body to the [consume] callback. Do not store
the reader outside that callback. Buffering helpers such as
[Object.Buffer.get_string ~max_size] keep response-size limits explicit.

{[
let consume reader =
  let bytes = Bytes.create 8192 in
  match Awskit_s3_eio.Runtime.read reader bytes ~off:0 ~len:8192 with
  | Error _ as error -> error
  | Ok n -> Ok (Bytes.sub_string bytes 0 n)
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.get s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large.bin"
  ~consume
  ()
]}

Use [Object.head] when only metadata is needed. Use [Object.exists] for boolean
existence checks. Missing-object and missing-bucket behavior is reported as
structured service errors by primitive operations.

{2 Copy, Delete, List, Tagging}

The object surface also includes:

{ul
  {li [Object.delete] and [Object.delete_many]}
  {li [Object.copy]}
  {li [Object.list] for rich pages and [Object.list_keys] for key-only pages}
  {li [Object.list_versions] for [ListObjectVersions] pages, including object
    versions and delete markers}
  {li [Object.Paginator.pages], [objects], and [keys] for following
    [ListObjectsV2] continuation tokens}
  {li [Object.Versions.pages], [object_versions], and [delete_markers] for
    following version-listing markers}
  {li [Object.Tagging.get], [put], and [delete]}
}

Paginator helpers preserve the regular [Object.List.options] record and update
only the continuation token between requests. Use [max_pages] to cap traversal:

{[
let keys =
  Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Paginator.keys s3
    ~bucket:"my-bucket"
    ~options:{ Awskit_s3.Object.List.default_options with prefix = Some "logs/" }
    ~max_pages:10
    ()
]}

{2 Versioning}

[Bucket.Versioning.put] enables bucket versioning. Object writes, copies, and
multipart completion return version IDs when the bucket is versioned. Reads,
heads, and deletes accept a [version_id] option. Copy requests can select a
source version with [Object.Copy.source_version_id].

Deleting the current object in a versioned bucket creates a delete marker.
[Object.list_versions] returns object versions and delete markers, and
[Object.Versions] provides paginator helpers for traversing the full history:

{[
let options =
  {
    Awskit_s3.Object.Versions.default_options with
    prefix = Some "logs/";
  }
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Versions.object_versions s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~options
  ()
]}

{1 Object.Buffer}

[Object.Buffer] is for explicit in-memory use. Upload helpers accept strings or
bytes. Download helpers require [max_size] to avoid accidental unbounded
buffering.

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Buffer.put_string s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  "hello"
]}

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Buffer.get_string s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  ~max_size:1_048_576L
  ()
]}

If the response body exceeds [max_size], the helper returns
[Error (Awskit.Error.Body _)].

{1 Object.Transfer}

Unix-capable adapters expose local-path helpers under [Object.Transfer].
Keeping these helpers at the adapter layer lets the pure [awskit-s3] core stay
independent of filesystem APIs.

{2 Eio}

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Transfer.upload_from_path s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"archive.tar"
  ~on_progress:(fun bytes -> Fmt.pr "uploaded %Ld bytes@." bytes)
  ~path
  ()
]}

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Transfer.download_to_path s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"archive.tar"
  ~on_progress:(fun bytes -> Fmt.pr "downloaded %Ld bytes@." bytes)
  ~path
  ()
]}

{2 Lwt Unix}

{[
let* result =
  Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.Object.Transfer.upload_from_path s3
    ~bucket:"my-bucket"
    ~key:"archive.tar"
    ~path:"/tmp/archive.tar"
    ()
]}

For larger files, use the multipart path helper. It uploads file parts with
bounded concurrency and aborts the multipart upload if a fresh upload fails:

{[
let* result =
  Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.Object.Transfer.upload_multipart_from_path s3
    ~bucket:"my-bucket"
    ~key:"large.tar"
    ~path:"/tmp/large.tar"
    ~concurrency:4
    ~on_progress:(fun bytes -> Fmt.pr "uploaded %Ld bytes@." bytes)
    ()
]}

To resume an interrupted multipart upload, keep the [upload_id]. The resume
helper lists uploaded parts, skips matching part numbers and sizes, uploads the
missing parts, and completes the upload. Existing uploads are left open on
failure, so the same upload ID can be retried:

{[
let* result =
  Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.Object.Transfer.resume_multipart_upload_from_path s3
    ~bucket:"my-bucket"
    ~key:"large.tar"
    ~upload_id
    ~path:"/tmp/large.tar"
    ()
]}

{1 AWS S3 Endpoint Configuration}

S3 endpoint configuration is part of client construction. The defaults use the
standard regional AWS S3 endpoint for the selected region.

{[
let s3 =
  Awskit_s3_eio.create
    ~sw
    ~env
    ~region
    ~credentials
    ~endpoint_variant:`Dualstack
    ~addressing_style:`Auto
    ()
]}

Pass [endpoint] for S3 endpoints that are not the default AWS regional host,
including local test services:

{[
let endpoint =
  Awskit.Endpoint.of_string_exn "http://127.0.0.1:9000"
in
let s3 =
  Awskit_s3_eio.create
    ~sw
    ~env
    ~region
    ~credentials
    ~endpoint
    ~addressing_style:`Path
    ()
]}

Endpoint variants:

{ul
  {li [`Regional] — [s3.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Dualstack] — [s3.dualstack.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Fips] — [s3-fips.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Fips_dualstack] — [s3-fips.dualstack.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Accelerate] — [s3-accelerate.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Accelerate_dualstack] — [s3-accelerate.dualstack.amazonaws.com]}
}

Addressing styles:

{ul
  {li [`Virtual_hosted] puts the bucket in the host name.}
  {li [`Path] puts the bucket in the path.}
  {li [`Auto] uses virtual-hosted style when valid and falls back to path-style
    for dotted bucket names over HTTPS.}
}

Transfer Acceleration requires virtual-hosted-style-compatible bucket names and
must be enabled on the bucket in AWS.

{1 Bucket Operations}

[Bucket] covers general-purpose bucket basics and the first configuration
subresources:

{ul
  {li [Bucket.create], [delete], [head], [exists], [list], [get_location]}
  {li [Bucket.Policy.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Policy_status.get]}
  {li [Bucket.Versioning.get], [put]}
  {li [Bucket.Tagging.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Encryption.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Cors.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Website.get], [put], [delete] for index/error documents}
  {li [Bucket.Public_access_block.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Ownership_controls.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Request_payment.get], [put]}
  {li [Bucket.Accelerate.get], [put]}
  {li [Bucket.Logging.get], [put] for target bucket/prefix logging}
}

[Bucket.Policy] sends and receives validated JSON policy payloads for bucket
access configuration. Applications can construct those documents with their own
policy model and pass the serialized JSON to the client.

{[
let encryption =
  {
    Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.rules =
      [
        {
          Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.Rule.sse_algorithm =
            Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.Algorithm.Aes256;
          kms_master_key_id = None;
        };
      ];
  }
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Encryption.put s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  encryption
]}

{1 Multipart Uploads}

[Multipart] exposes the primitive AWS multipart flow:

{ol
  {li [Multipart.create] starts an upload and returns an upload ID.}
  {li [Multipart.upload_part] uploads a known-length part.}
  {li [Multipart.complete] completes the upload with ordered parts.}
  {li [Multipart.abort] cancels an upload.}
  {li [Multipart.list_parts] inspects one page of uploaded parts.}
  {li [Multipart.Paginator.pages] and [parts] follow [ListParts] part-number
    markers.}
}

[Multipart.upload_part] follows the same upload body contract as [Object.put]:
parts require a known exact length, and custom stream producer errors are part
of the upload result.

[Multipart.Managed.upload_string] and [upload_bytes] provide a higher-level
sequential multipart upload helper. The helper creates the upload, splits the
body into AWS-safe parts, uploads each part, completes the upload, and attempts
to abort if a part upload or completion fails. Defaults use 8 MiB parts, with
the AWS minimum 5 MiB enforced for configured part sizes.

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Multipart.Managed.upload_string s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large.bin"
  large_body
]}

{1 Presigned URLs}

Use adapter-level [Presigned] modules when a connection already exists, or use
standalone {!module:Awskit_s3.Presigned} helpers when generating URLs without
an adapter connection.

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Presigned.get_object s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"public.txt"
  ()
]}

{[
Awskit_s3.Presigned.put_object
  ~region
  ~credentials
  ~now
  ~endpoint
  ~addressing_style:`Path
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"upload.txt"
  ()
]}

Multipart direct-upload flows can presign individual [UploadPart] requests
after [Multipart.create] has produced an upload ID:

{[
Awskit_s3.Presigned.upload_part
  ~region
  ~credentials
  ~now
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large.bin"
  ~upload_id
  ~part_number:1
  ()
]}

Presigned URLs use [UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD] and the same endpoint/addressing options
as normal operations.

A typical direct-upload multipart flow is:

{ol
  {li Start the upload with [Multipart.create].}
  {li Presign one [UploadPart] URL per part number with
    [Presigned.upload_part].}
  {li The uploader sends each part to the corresponding URL.}
  {li Complete the upload with the returned part ETags.}
}

{1 Credentials}

Eio S3 connections require explicit [region] and [credentials].

{[
let credentials =
  Awskit.Credentials.create_exn
    ~access_key_id:"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
    ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
    ()
in
let region = Awskit.Region.of_string_exn "us-east-1" in
Awskit_s3_eio.create ~sw ~env ~region ~credentials ()
]}

[awskit-s3-lwt-unix] can use explicit values or load the standard environment
variables through [awskit-lwt-unix]:

{[
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
AWS_REGION
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
AWS_PROFILE
AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE
AWS_CONFIG_FILE
AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_RELATIVE_URI
AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_FULL_URI
AWS_CONTAINER_AUTHORIZATION_TOKEN
AWS_CONTAINER_AUTHORIZATION_TOKEN_FILE
]}

{[
Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.create ()
]}

The Unix adapters cover static credentials, environment variables, shared AWS
config files, named static profiles, ECS container credentials, and EC2
instance profile credentials. Metadata credentials are resolved through the
Lwt-Unix runtime credential source and refreshed before expiration.

{1 Errors}

S3 operations return [(value, Awskit_s3.Error.t) result] or that result inside
the selected runtime monad.

[Awskit_s3.Error.t] is {!type:Awskit.Error.t}. It preserves validation,
signing, transport, service, decode, and body-consumption failures.

Use S3 classifiers for service-specific checks:

{[
match result with
| Ok value -> use value
| Error err when Awskit_s3.Error.is_no_such_key err -> handle_missing ()
| Error err when Awskit_s3.Error.is_precondition_failed err -> retry_later ()
| Error err -> log_error err
]}

For service failures, [Awskit_s3.Error.service_code] exposes the AWS error
code when present.

{1 Retries}

Runtime-backed S3 clients use {!val:Awskit.Retry.default} unless a runtime
adapter receives a custom [retry_policy]. Retries are centralized around the
signed S3 request, so retryable service responses are classified before being
returned to callers.

Only replayable upload bodies are retried. In-memory bodies created with
[Runtime.string_body] and [Runtime.bytes_body] are replayable. Custom stream
bodies are retried only when their {!type:Awskit.Body.Upload.descriptor}
marks them replayable. Do not mark a stream replayable unless retrying can
recreate the same bytes from the beginning.

Configure retry attempts, capped exponential backoff, and jitter explicitly:

{[
let retry_policy =
  Awskit.Retry.create_exn
    ~max_attempts:4
    ~base_delay:(Ptime.Span.of_float_s 0.2 |> Option.get)
    ~max_delay:(Ptime.Span.of_float_s 3.0 |> Option.get)
    ~jitter:0.5
    ()
in
Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.create ~retry_policy ()
]}

[jitter] is in the range [[0, 1]]. The default [0] keeps deterministic delays.
Values greater than zero spread retry waits below the capped exponential delay.

{1 Local MinIO Contract Testing}

Normal tests use the deterministic in-memory simulator and do not require a
network service. For local wire-level checks against MinIO, start the included
Compose stack and run the opt-in alias:

{[
docker compose up -d
dune build @minio-contract
]}

The contract runner defaults to [http://127.0.0.1:9000] with the
[minioadmin]/[minioadmin] credentials from [docker-compose.yml]. Override with
[AWSKIT_S3_MINIO_ENDPOINT], [AWSKIT_S3_MINIO_ACCESS_KEY_ID],
[AWSKIT_S3_MINIO_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY], and [AWSKIT_S3_MINIO_REGION].

{1 Simulator Testing}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Sim} exposes the same synchronous S3 operation shape as the
runtime-backed client and adds test-only helpers:

{ul
  {li deterministic clock}
  {li in-memory store inspection}
  {li operation history}
  {li deterministic fault injection}
}

{[
let clock = Awskit_s3.Sim.Clock.create () in
let store = Awskit_s3.Sim.create_store ~clock () in
let conn = Awskit_s3.Sim.connect store ~credentials in

ignore
  (Awskit_s3.Sim.Bucket.create conn ~bucket:"test-bucket" ()
   |> Result.get_ok);

ignore
  (Awskit_s3.Sim.Object.Buffer.put_string conn
     ~bucket:"test-bucket"
     ~key:"hello.txt"
     "hello"
   |> Result.get_ok)
]}

[Sim.Response_lost] is modeled as a body-read failure. [Sim.Slow_down],
[Sim.Internal_error], and [Sim.Connection_reset] fail object and multipart
operations before mutation. The simulator models enabled bucket versioning for
object puts, gets, heads, deletes, copies, multipart completion, and delete
markers. Suspended bucket versioning models the S3 [null] object version and
[null] delete marker behavior.
